          "chunk_id": "060713a31bedeb78:part:003"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "mechanism_2",
      "name": "dislocation-twin boundary interaction",
      "category": "deformation mechanism",
      "sample_id": "sample_1",
      "material_state": "deformed at 600°C",
      "assertion_level": "author_interpretation",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": "12",
          "section": null,
          "quote": "It is also observed in Fig. 8 b that the dislocations are confined within twin boundaries. This interplay between twin boundaries and dislocations plays a crucial role in sustaining work hardening while simultaneously inhibiting softening.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": "8b",
          "chunk_id": "060713a31bedeb78:part:003"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "mechanism_3",
      "name": "grain boundary bulging",
      "category": "dynamic recrystallization",
      "sample_id": "sample_1",
      "material_state": "deformed at 600°C",
      "assertion_level": "author_interpretation",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": "12",
          "section": null,
          "quote": "Another distinct feature is bulging of grain boundaries to facilitate dynamic recrystallization. The grain boundary bulging is a common feature for materials deformed at high temperatures [ 67 ]. This GB bulging acts as stress reliever during deformation to accommodate steady state deformation without fracture as seen in current work at high temperatures.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "060713a31bedeb78:part:003"
        }
      ]
    },
